                  "text": "## Summary\nZone resilience (also called zone redundancy or zone-resilient) means designing Azure services so they continue to operate if a single Availability Zone inside a region fails. Zone-resilient services replicate compute or data across multiple zones in the same Azure region so an outage in one zone does not cause a full service outage.\n\n## Key points\n- Definition: Replication or distribution of resources across two or more Azure Availability Zones so a single-zone failure does not take the service offline.\n\n- How it works: Services use synchronous or active-active replication across zones, automatic traffic routing to healthy zone replicas, and redundant instances so workloads remain available during a zone outage.\n\n- What it does and does not protect against:\n  - Protects against datacenter/zone-level failures inside a region.\n  - Does NOT protect against a full region outage — for region-level protection use geo-replication or geo-redundant options.\n\n- Examples (service-specific behavior):\n  - Azure Storage with Zone-Redundant Storage (ZRS) keeps data copies synchronously in three zones for zero data-loss on a single-zone failure: [Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/storage/common/storage-redundancy#zone-redundant-storage)\n  - App Service Plans can distribute worker instances across zones so apps keep serving traffic if one zone fails: [Reliability in App Service](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/reliability/reliability-app-service#resilience-to-availability-zone-failures)\n  - Event Hubs namespaces can be zone-redundant so event data and metadata are available across zones: [Reliability in Azure Event Hubs](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/reliability/reliability-event-hubs#resilience-to-availability-zone-failures)\n\n- Benefits: improved availability, reduced single-point-of-failure risk, better tolerance for datacenter-level incidents.\n\n- Practical considerations / best practices:\n- Verify the service and SKU support zone redundancy and choose zone-capable SKUs.\n- Design clients and apps for transient errors and retries (idempotent operations when possible).\n- Test failure scenarios (chaos or simulated zone failure tests) to validate behavior.\n- For protection against region-wide disasters, combine zone resilience with geo-replication or cross-region DR.\n- Review cost and configuration trade-offs before enabling zone-redundant options.\n\n## How I can help\nI can assist with:\n- **Checking and improving zonal resiliency for your Azure resources and service groups**\n- **Creating, managing, and querying Azure Service Groups**\n- **Managing zonal resiliency goals for service groups**\n- **Evaluating application resilience and generating zone-resilient Bicep or ARM templates**\n\nWould you like me to check the zonal resiliency posture for a specific service or service group, or run an assessment for your application?"
                }
